    "Nc6"
   ],
   "spoken": "The knight to c6 is the main line — and here is the pitfall: the natural knight to f3 is a mistake. It walks straight into the capture on d4 followed by the bishop pin on g4, dropping the e5-pawn. Nor does the knight to e2 help — it blocks the bishop.",
   "kind": "trap",
   "teaches": "In the Four Pawns, Nf3 too early loses e5 to Nxd4 and Bg4 — develop the other pieces first.",
   "plans": "Defend e5 another way before committing the king's knight."
  },
  {
   "ply": 13,
   "t": 104,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/ppp1pppp/1nn5/4P3/2PP4/4B3/PP4PP/RN1QKBNR b KQkq - 2 7",
   "line": [
    "Be3"
   ],
   "spoken": "The right way to hold e5 without allowing the pin is the bishop to e3. It defends the centre and keeps the king's knight free to come out only once it is safe.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": "Defend with a developing move that doesn't create a pin — the bishop to e3 holds the centre.",
   "plans": "Develop the queen's knight, then the king's knight."
  },
